{"id":"c327ec5c-2013-4d9a-8f47-5a7a2912bd67","object":"chat.completion","created":1784991181,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    trials = case['trials']\\n    memory = []\\n    failure_map = {}\\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        sig = trial['signature']\\n        ref = trial['reflection']\\n        succ = trial['success']\\n        if not succ:\\n            if sig in failure_map:\\n                pos = failure_map[sig]\\n                memory[pos]['reflection'] = ref\\n                memory[pos]['last_trial'] = idx\\n            else:\\n                entry = {'last_trial': idx, 'reflection': ref, 'signature': sig, 'success': False}\\n                memory.append(entry)\\n                failure_map[sig] = len(memory) - 1\\n        else:\\n            memory.append({'last_trial': idx, 'reflection': ref, 'signature': sig, 'success': True})\\n    if len(memory) > capacity:\\n        failures = [e for e in memory if not e['success']]\\n        successes = [e for e in memory if e['success']]\\n        def sort_key(e):\\n            return (-e['last_trial'], e['signature'])\\n        failures.sort(key=sort_key)\\n        successes.sort(key=sort_key)\\n        kept = []\\n        failures_take = min(len(failures), capacity)\\n        kept.extend(failures[:failures_take])\\n        remaining = capacity - len(kept)\\n        if remaining > 0:\\n            successes_take = min(len(successes), remaining)\\n            kept.extend(successes[:successes_take])\\n        kept.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\\n        memory = kept\\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in memory]\\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for AGENT-RF-01. The task: given a case payload with capacity and trials (list of objects with signature, reflection, success), we need to maintain a memory of entries (each with last_trial, reflection, signature, success) and a list of all signatures (in order? of all trials? Or just of the memory?). The output schema: object with \"memory\" (array of objects with last_trial, reflection, signature, success) and \"signatures\" (array of strings). \n\nThe description: \"Reflexion episodic memory bookkeeping and prompt assembly; free-form reflection generation is supplied.\" It says: \"Process trials in chronological order. Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection. When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature. Restore chronological order in the final memory. Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\"\n\nAlso: \"treat these registered properties as hard contracts: feedback_provenance, memory_bound, stable_order, no_weight_update.\" So we must ensure stable order after truncation, etc.\n\nThe fixture shows payload with capacity (integer) and trials array. We need to simulate episodic memory: start with empty memory. For each trial in order, we incorporate it. The memory should contain entries for each distinct signature? But deduplicate by failure signature: only failures? Or all? \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" Means: if we have an entry for a signature that is a failure (success=false), and we encounter another trial with same signature (failure), we replace with the newer reflection? Or do we also include successes? The final memory includes both failures and successes? The output schema requires \"success\" boolean. So entries can be both. The deduplication is only for failure signatures? Or for all? The text: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" That implies only when the new trial is a failure and the existing entry is also failure? Possibly we keep only the most recent reflection for each failure signature. For successes, we may keep multiple? Possibly we keep all trials? But then \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" That suggests we have a mix of failures and successes, and when exceeding capacity, we keep failures first, then among those, keep the most recent ones, then by signature for ties? Then restore chronological order (presumably the order of trials in the input? Or the order after dedup? The final memory should be in chronological order of the trials that were retained, i.e., the order they were added? Possibly we need to maintain insertion order? But after truncation, we need to preserve the order of the retained trials.\n\nLet's parse: we process trials in order. Initially memory empty. For each trial (signature s, reflection r, success b), we check if we already have an entry with same signature and b==False (failure). If yes, replace that entry with new reflection, update last_trial to current trial index (0-based?), and set success (should be false). If the new trial is a success (b==True), we add it as a new entry? But then deduplication says \"by failure signature\" only. So successes are not deduplicated; each success adds a new entry? But then when capacity binds, we prioritize failures before successes. That would mean we keep all failures, then as many successes as possible. Then among those, recency and signature. Let's read carefully: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" That order: first failures, then successes. Within failures, recency (most recent?) and then signature (maybe alphabetical?). For recency, we need to define: probably most recent trial index. Then signature for tie-breaking. Then after truncation, restore chronological order (so sort by last_trial ascending? or by the order of trials as they were added? Since we process chronologically, adding entries in order, the last_trial is the index of the trial that created/updated the entry. So if we truncate and then sort by last_trial ascending, that gives chronological order. But we also might have updated entries with later last_trial, so they move to later position. That's fine.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Case has capacity (k) and trials list.\n- We'll assign each trial an index (0-based) as we process.\n- We'll maintain a list memory_entries (each a dict: last_trial, reflection, signature, success). Also possibly a dict for quick lookup by failure signature.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. Initialize empty list entries = [].\n2. For each trial in trials with index i:\n   - s = trial['signature']; r = trial['reflection']; b = trial['success'].\n   - If b is False:\n        - Check if we already have an entry with same signature and success=False in entries. If yes, update that entry: reflection = r; last_trial = i; success remains False (unchanged). Also we might need to update the failure lookup dict.\n        - Else, add new entry: {last_trial: i, reflection: r, signature: s, success: False}.\n   - Else (b True):\n        - Add new entry: {last_trial: i, reflection: r, signature: s, success: True}. No dedup for successes.\n3. After processing all trials, we have a list of entries (some may have been updated multiple times). Now we need to enforce capacity k.\n   - If len(entries) <= k: keep all. Else truncate.\n   - To truncate: separate failures (success=False) and successes (success=True). \n   - For failures: we want to keep all? But if there are more failures than capacity, we need to truncate failures first? The instruction: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" That means we should keep all failures, then if capacity remains, keep successes. But if failures exceed capacity? It says prioritize failures, but failures themselves also need to be truncated if too many. The priority: first keep failures, then successes. So if failures count > capacity, we keep only the most recent failures (by recency, then signature) and no successes. If failures count <= capacity, keep all failures, then fill remaining slots with successes, again using recency and signature for selection.\n   - For each group, we need to sort by recency and signature: recency meaning higher last_trial (more recent) is better? Or lower? The phrase \"prioritize ... recency and signature\" suggests that when selecting, we prefer more recent entries (higher last_trial) and if tie, maybe alphabetical signature order? We need to decide. Usually \"recency\" means more recent is higher priority. So we sort descending by last_trial, then ascending by signature? Or descending? To get deterministic order. We'll sort descending by last_trial (so most recent first), then ascending by signature (e.g., 'e0' < 'e1' etc.). Then take top entries from each group accordingly.\n   - After selecting which entries to keep, we need to restore chronological order: sort the kept entries by last_trial ascending (since we want order of trial occurrence).\n4. Also we need to produce signatures list: probably the list of all signatures from the original trials in order? Or from the memory? The description: \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" The output schema includes \"signatures\" array of strings. It likely means the signatures of all trials that were processed? Or the signatures of the retained entries? The schema has both memory and signatures as separate lists. The signatures array could be the list of signatures from the original trials (in order) for prompt assembly? But the requirement says \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" The retained entries are in memory. The signatures might be just the signatures of those retained entries? But then that would be redundant. Alternatively, it could be the list of all distinct signatures encountered? Or all signatures from the trials? Let's look at typical Reflexion: memory is a list of experiences (each has signature, reflection, success, last_trial). Signatures might be a separate list of all failure signatures for quick retrieval? Or for constructing prompt? The output schema says \"signatures\" is an array of strings, not necessarily tied to memory directly. Possibly it's the list of signatures from the trials that were used to generate the memory? For example, in the fixture, the trials have signatures, some repeated. The expected output might include all signatures in order of trials, or only those that are in memory? Hard to know.\n\nLet's examine the fixture inputs to infer. The fixture shows three cases with capacity 3 and 7 trials each. We cannot see expected output, but we can reason from the algorithm. The description: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" So only failure signatures are deduplicated. Successes are added individually. So memory may contain multiple entries for the same success signature? Possibly. Then truncation.\n\nTo decide on \"signatures\" output, let's read the task again: \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" That suggests the signatures list is the list of signatures for the retained entries (in order). But the output schema has both memory and signatures as separate arrays. That would be redundant but allowed. Another possibility: signatures is a list of all signatures that appear in the trials (maybe in order of first occurrence?) or all distinct signatures? The word \"their\" could refer to the retained entries, i.e., the signatures of those entries. So signatures list should have same length as memory, each entry corresponds to the same index. But that would be redundant with the signature field in each memory entry. However, the schema does not require uniqueness or mapping. Could also be a separate flattened list for convenience. The typical Reflexion paper (or the original implementation) uses a list of signatures to define the order for querying? Hard to know.\n\nGiven the strict output schema: it's a JSON object with \"memory\" array of objects and \"signatures\" array of strings. The \"signatures\" array is required but not described further. It could just be the list of signatures from all trials in input order? But then it would include duplicates. Or maybe it's the list of failure signatures retained? Let's consider the context: \"Reflexion episodic memory bookkeeping and prompt assembly; free-form reflection generation is supplied.\" So the signatures might be used as a key to retrieve reflections. Possibly the output's signatures list should be the signatures of all trial outcomes, but deduplicated? The input has a list of all trials. The algorithm processes them. The output memory and signatures likely represent the state after processing. Since the memory is the retained entries, and each entry has a signature, we can just output the signatures list as the signature of each memory entry in order. That seems natural: the signatures of the retained entries.\n\nBut the fixture's payload includes many examples. Without expected output, we must rely on the description.\n\nLet's think about typical Reflexion memory: an agent maintains a list of experiences (trial outcomes). The memory has a fixed capacity. The agent uses the memory to generate reflections. The signatures are used to index experiences. The \"signatures\" array might be the list of all signatures that have been seen, perhaps for a separate query? Or it could be the list of signatures for which we have a reflection (i.e., the keys in a dictionary). In the description: \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" It doesn't say \"a list of all signatures\". So I'll assume \"their signatures\" means the signatures of the retained entries. So signatures list will have the same length as memory, in the same order.\n\nAlternatively, maybe signatures is simply the list of signatures from the input trials in order? Because they might want to know the order of attempts. But then it would include duplicates and that seems less useful.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll choose to output signatures as the list of signatures from the retained entries, in the same order as the memory entries. This matches the phrase \"their signatures\" (possessive of \"retained entries\"). Also, it's easy to generate.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Process trials as described.\n- After truncation and chronological sort, produce memory list.\n- Extract signatures list from memory.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). It receives the payload: {\"capacity\": int, \"trials\": list}. We need to return a dict with \"memory\" and \"signatures\".\n\nCheck for empty cases: if no trials, memory empty, signatures empty.\n\nAlso note: The case may have additional fields like \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate the case.\n\nNow, write Python code.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Use zero-based index as trial number.\n- For failure dedup: maintain a dict that maps failure signature to index in entries list. But since we update in place, we can update the entry and also update its last_trial. For the truncation step, we need to know the current list of entries. We'll keep a list 'memory' and a dict 'failure_idx'.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    capacity = case['capacity']\n    trials = case['trials']\n    memory = []  # list of dicts\n    failure_map = {}  # signature -> index in memory (only for failure entries)\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        sig = trial['signature']\n        ref = trial['reflection']\n        succ = trial['success']\n        if not succ:  # failure\n            if sig in failure_map:\n                idx = failure_map[sig]\n                memory[idx]['reflection'] = ref\n                memory[idx]['last_trial'] = i\n            else:\n                entry = {'last_trial': i, 'reflection': ref, 'signature': sig, 'success': False}\n                memory.append(entry)\n                failure_map[sig] = len(memory)-1\n        else:  # success\n            memory.append({'last_trial': i, 'reflection': ref, 'signature': sig, 'success': True})\n    # truncation\n    if len(memory) > capacity:\n        # separate\n        failures = [e for e in memory if not e['success']]\n        successes = [e for e in memory if e['success']]\n        # sort each group by recency desc, then signature asc\n        def sort_key(e):\n            return (-e['last_trial'], e['signature'])\n        failures.sort(key=sort_key)\n        successes.sort(key=sort_key)\n        # select\n        kept = []\n        # take all failures if possible, but if more failures than capacity, take top failures\n        failures_to_take = min(len(failures), capacity)\n        kept.extend(failures[:failures_to_take])\n        remaining = capacity - len(kept)\n        if remaining > 0:\n            successes_to_take = min(len(successes), remaining)\n            kept.extend(successes[:successes_to_take])\n        # now restore chronological order: sort by last_trial ascending\n        kept.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\n        memory = kept\n    # build signatures list\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in memory]\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\n\nBut we need to check: when we update a failure entry, the old entry's index in memory remains, but its order in the list may be not chronological. That's fine because we later may reorder during truncation and final sort. However, note that when we update a failure entry, we change its last_trial to current index, which may be larger than previous. That affects recency. The order in the memory list after update is not chronological (since we are updating in place, the entry stays at its original position). But we only care about the final list after truncation and sorting by last_trial. So it's fine.\n\nPotential issue: When we update a failure entry, we also need to consider that the new reflection may have the same signature but the success flag is still false. That's okay.\n\nEdge case: If we update a failure entry, and later that entry gets truncated away because capacity binds, it's fine.\n\nBut there might be a nuance: The dedup is \"retaining the newest reflection.\" So we replace the reflection with the newest one.\n\nNow, ensure type checking: The output schema requires integer last_trial, string reflection, string signature, boolean success. We need to ensure we output those types. The input provides them as such.\n\nAlso, we must not include any additional keys.\n\nNow, implement the function and return as part of implementation string.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes for JSON.\n\nWe'll write the code as a single function.\n\nTesting mentally with first fixture case:\n\ncase0:\ntrials:\n0: e1, r0, True\n1: e3, r1, False\n2: e0, r2, True\n3: e1, r3, False\n4: e3, r4, False\n5: e1, r5, True\n6: e0, r6, False\ncapacity=3\n\nProcess:\ni0: success e1 -> add (last_trial=0, r0, e1, True)\ni1: failure e3 -> add (1, r1, e3, False); failure_map[e3]=1\ni2: success e0 -> add (2, r2, e0, True)\ni3: failure e1 -> (e1 not in failure_map) -> add (3, r3, e1, False); failure_map[e1]=3\ni4: failure e3 -> e3 in failure_map (index1) -> update memory[1] reflection r4, last_trial=4.\n memory now: [ (0, r0, e1, T), (4, r4, e3, F), (2, r2, e0, T), (3, r3, e1, F) ]\ni5: success e1 -> add (5, r5, e1, T)\ni6: failure e0 -> e0 not in failure_map -> add (6, r6, e0, False); failure_map[e0]=5 (since index after adding? Actually memory length before i6 is 5, after i5 it's 5 entries? Let's recount: after i4 memory has 4 entries. i5 adds success: memory length 5. i6 adds failure: length 6. So indices 0-5. failure_map after i4: {e3:1, e1:3}. i5 not failure. i6: e0 not in map, add at index5 (len after appending), so failure_map[e0]=5.\nNow memory (6 entries):\n0: T e1 last0\n1: F e3 last4 (updated)\n2: T e0 last2\n3: F e1 last3\n4: T e1 last5\n5: F e0 last6\n\nNow truncate to 3.\nSeparate failures: entries 1,3,5 (all failures). successes: 0,2,4.\nSort failures by recency desc: last_trial: 6 (idx5), 4 (idx1), 3 (idx3) -> order after sort: [last6 F e0, last4 F e3, last3 F e1]. Also signature tie? Not needed. So failures list: [F e0, F e3, F e1].\nSuccesses sort: last5 T e1, last2 T e0, last0 T e1 -> order: [T e1 last5, T e0 last2, T e1 last0] (T e1 last5, then last2, then last0). Actually last5 > last2 > last0, so descending.\nSince capacity=3, failures_to_take = min(3,3)=3, so we take all failures: [F e0, F e3, F e1]. remaining=0, so no successes.\nThen chronological order by last_trial ascending: last3 F e1 (last3), last4 F e3 (last4), last6 F e0 (last6). So memory: \n[ {last_trial:3, reflection:r3, signature:e1, success:false},\n  {last_trial:4, reflection:r4, signature:e3, success:false},\n  {last_trial:6, reflection:r6, signature:e0, success:false} ]\n\nSignatures: ['e1','e3','e0'].\n\nSeems plausible.\n\nNow second case: need to test but we'll trust.\n\nOne more nuance: When we have failure dedup, we only deduplicate on failure signatures. That means if we have a success with same signature, it doesn't affect the failure entry. So we might have both a failure and a success with same signature in memory. That's allowed.\n\nNow about the order of processing: The description says \"Process trials in chronological order.\" We did.\n\nNow, we need to output the implementation as a string inside JSON. Ensure we escape properly.\n\nWe'll write the Python code as a function solve(case) that returns the dictionary.\n\nAlso note: The input case may contain other keys like \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1607,"completion_tokens":4967,"total_tokens":6574,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4534},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":71},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}